From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rm: free removed directories and keep link counts correct

rm_file() decrements i_links_count once and frees the inode only when the
count reaches zero. That is right for a regular file, which starts at 1,
and wrong for a directory, which starts at 2: one link for its entry in the
parent, one for its own ".". The count lands on 1, delete_file() never
runs, and the directory is unlinked but never freed.

e2rm -r therefore leaves an orphan behind:

    Unconnected directory inode 13 (was in /)
    '..' in ... (13) is / (2), should be <The NULL inode> (0).

The parent is left wrong too: it carries a link for the child's "..", which
nothing gives back, so its i_links_count stays one too high.

Zero the count for a directory and drop the parent's link with it.
delete_file() then frees the blocks and the inode as it does for a file.

Free the inode with ext2fs_inode_alloc_stats2() while here, passing the
isdir flag, so the group descriptor's used_dirs_count is decremented too --
ext2fs_inode_alloc_stats() cannot know and always leaves it high.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/src/util.c b/src/util.c
--- a/src/util.c
+++ b/src/util.c
@@ -174,7 +174,27 @@ rm_file(ext2_filsys fs, ext2_ino_t cwd, char *outfile, ext2_ino_t delfile)
   if ((retval = read_inode(fs, delfile, &inode)))
     return(retval);

-  --inode.i_links_count;
+  if (LINUX_S_ISDIR(inode.i_mode))
+    {
+      /* a directory holds a link to itself through ".", and its parent holds
+         one more for the child's ".."; neither survives the removal */
+      struct ext2_inode parent;
+
+      inode.i_links_count = 0;
+
+      if ((retval = read_inode(fs, cwd, &parent)))
+        return(retval);
+
+      if (parent.i_links_count > 1)
+        {
+          --parent.i_links_count;
+          if ((retval = write_inode(fs, cwd, &parent)))
+            return(retval);
+        }
+    }
+  else
+    --inode.i_links_count;
+
   if ((retval = write_inode(fs, delfile, &inode)))
     return(retval);

@@ -216,7 +236,8 @@ delete_file(ext2_filsys fs, ext2_ino_t inode)
       return(retval);
     }

-  ext2fs_inode_alloc_stats(fs, inode, -1);
+  ext2fs_inode_alloc_stats2(fs, inode, -1,
+                            LINUX_S_ISDIR(inode_buf.i_mode) ? 1 : 0);

   return(0);
 }
